  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nFall-like pattern is firmly entrenched into the region behind\nyesterday's cold front and today's continued weak cold air\nadvection. Aloft, a zonal jet moves through offering some weak\nforcing for ascent in southeast ND, bringing a low chance for a\nband of light rain to this area. However, there is dry air it \nwill need to overcome, and thus expecting not much rainfall from\nthis - less than two tenths of an inch.\n\nSurface high pressure into the region Sunday will keep\nconditions pleasant and fall-like with high temperatures again\nin the 60s, along with ample sunshine and light winds.\n\nA stronger upper shortwave trough turning closed low moves\nthrough the northern High Plains into south-central Canada on\nMonday. This will bring a plume of moisture and forcing as it\napproaches the region to the majority of the Dakotas and\nMinnesota. This will also bring gusty winds both ahead and\nbehind the wave - highest at least 40 mph likely within eastern\nND into the Red River Valley.\n\nLooking into mid to late next week, ensemble's evolution of the \nupper pattern shows signs of upper ridging, indicating at\ntemperature trend that may gradually increase. However, the lack\nof more robust upper ridging means we should not see a drastic\nincreased trend in temperatures, likely keeping them closer to\naverage to slightly above average.\n\n...High chance for wetting rainfall...\n\nAll guidance agrees in a leading plume of moisture and lift\noverspreading our entire region on Monday, lending toward the\nthought of widespread stratiform rainfall for majority of the\narea. Within this plume will also likely be weak instability, \noffering convective elements to percolate within the widespread\nstratiform rainfall. This should bring at least a wetting \nrainfall to majority of the region (at least a couple of tenths \nof an inch). \n\nHowever, a recent trend in the heart of synoptic forcing via \nthe deformation zone/center of vorticity to keep it within \nCanada means this may be the only opportunity for widespread \nrainfall, while also limiting higher end amounts. While there is\na scenario that offers rainfall amounts more than 1 inch, they \nwill likely be more spotty/isolated due to convective elements \nneeded, if it occurs in our area at all.",
